<p>When a McAllen home or business is damaged by hail, wind, fire, or water, the repair has to meet the building codes in force today, not the codes that applied when the building went up. That gap between "how it was built" and "how it must be rebuilt" is a frequent source of confusion on insurance claims. This page summarizes McAllen building codes and how they interact with insurance claims, using only information published by the City of McAllen and other cited sources.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Last verified: October 3, 2026</span><p>Codes, fees, and procedures change. This page is general information, not code consulting or legal advice. Always confirm current requirements with the City of McAllen Building Permits &amp; Inspections department before starting work.</p></div>

<h2>Overview: McAllen adopted the 2024 ICC codes</h2>
<p>The City of McAllen adopted the <strong>2024 International Code Council (ICC) codes</strong> along with the <strong>2023 National Electrical Code</strong>, <strong>effective January 1, 2026</strong>. The adoption includes local exceptions covering interior residential lighting controls, a commercial adult changing-table requirement, and municipal residential fire sprinkler requirements. The City's Residential Building Permit Checklist (revised 01/2026) lists the International Residential Code 2024 and International Energy Code 2024, and a November 2025 Texas Border Business article quoted Chief Building Official Norma Yado, CPM, CBO, on the change.</p>

