<h2>Roofing repair and replacement</h2>
<p>Roofs are the most common place where codes and claims meet in McAllen. Composition shingle roofs dominate residential neighborhoods, while metal roofs are common on outbuildings and commercial properties, and low-slope or flat roofs are typical on commercial buildings. When a roof is repaired or replaced after hail or wind, the work generally needs a permit and must meet the current IRC or IBC roofing provisions, including requirements for underlayment, drip edge, and fastening.</p>
<p>Insurer estimates sometimes price the visible roofing material but leave out items that a permitted, code-compliant installation requires. Common examples to check include underlayment type, drip edge, starter and ridge components, and flashing. Whether those items are owed depends on whether they are part of repairing covered damage under your policy and on any ordinance or law coverage. For more on roof claims, see <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a>.</p>

<h2>Wind-resistant construction</h2>
<p>McAllen sees strong straight-line winds and occasional tropical systems. Wind requirements for buildings come from the adopted 2024 IRC and IBC and the standards they reference, such as ASCE 7, which assigns design wind speeds by location and risk category. We do not list a specific design wind speed here; your designer, contractor, or the City can confirm the value that applies to a particular project.</p>
<p>One point causes frequent confusion: the Texas Department of Insurance windstorm certification program (WPI-8) applies only within the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association catastrophe area, which includes coastal counties such as Cameron and Willacy. <strong>Hidalgo County is not in the TWIA area</strong>, so McAllen properties typically get wind and hail coverage from their regular insurer, and TDI windstorm certification does not apply. Local code wind provisions still do.</p>

<h2>Permit requirements</h2>
<p>Permits in McAllen are issued by the Building Permits &amp; Inspections department. The residential permit application includes Repair/Remodeling options. Key points from City documents:</p>
<ul>
<li>A permit becomes invalid if work does not begin within six months, and a permit is good for one year.</li>
<li>Under the fee schedule effective October 1, 2016, new construction, additions, and remodels are charged $0.16 per square foot, with a $48 minimum. Fees for other work types, including re-roofing, should be confirmed with the City.</li>
<li>Structures built before 1978 trigger the EPA lead-safe renovation rule disclosure.</li>
</ul>
<p>Permit costs for covered repairs are often part of a complete repair estimate. Keep copies of permits and fee receipts for your claim file.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Structural repairs</h2>
<p>Fire, wind, tree impact, and long-term water damage can affect framing, trusses, walls, and foundations. Structural repairs generally require permits, may require engineering, and must meet current code. NWS storm surveys of the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1084451" target="_blank" rel="noopener">April 28, 2023 wind event in McAllen</a>, for example, noted damage to weakly constructed roofs and a cinderblock wall without a bond beam. Repairing that kind of damage to current standards can cost more than restoring the original construction, which is where ordinance or law coverage becomes important. Because the International Existing Building Code is part of the adopted package, the extent of required upgrades during repairs may depend on the scope of the work; confirm with the City.</p>

<h2>Code-related upgrades</h2>
<p>Code-related upgrades are items that are not "damaged" in the usual sense but must be added or changed because the repair triggers current requirements. Examples can include electrical updates under the 2023 NEC when walls are opened, energy code items when insulation is replaced, or roofing components required by the 2024 IRC. Some upgrades apply only when certain thresholds are met. Ask the City or a licensed contractor which upgrades your specific project requires, and get that in writing where possible.</p>

<h2>Construction inspections</h2>
<p>Permitted work is inspected in stages. Common residential inspections in McAllen include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rough-in plumbing</li>
<li>Foundation</li>
<li>Framing</li>
<li>Insulation</li>
<li>Infiltration</li>
<li>Final</li>
</ul>
<p>Inspections can be requested at (956) 681-1328. A re-inspection fee of $48 applies when work must be inspected again. Inspection results and any correction notices are useful claim documents, because they show what the City required on the actual job.</p>

<h2>Contractor responsibilities</h2>
<p>In most projects, the contractor pulls the permit, builds to code, and schedules inspections. Before work starts, ask your contractor to confirm the permit, explain which code items are included in the price, and itemize code-required work separately from like-kind repairs. That separation makes it easier to present code items to your insurer. Remember that in Texas a public adjuster may not take part in repairing a property on a claim they adjust, so your contractor and your public adjuster play different roles.</p>

<h2>Ordinance or law coverage</h2>
<p>Ordinance or law coverage pays, up to its limit, for increased costs to comply with building codes when repairing covered damage. It is often a separate, limited coverage or an endorsement, and the limit may be a percentage of the dwelling coverage or a fixed amount. Some policies have little or none. Check your declarations page and policy forms, and note that some policies pay code-upgrade costs only after the work is actually completed. Our article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-mcallen/">building codes and insurance repairs in McAllen</a> explains this in more depth.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Code compliance is not the same as coverage</span><p>A requirement under McAllen's adopted codes does not automatically mean your insurer must pay for it. Payment for code upgrades depends on your policy's ordinance or law coverage, its limits, and its terms. Always read your own policy.</p></div>

<h2>Code-related claim disputes</h2>
<p>Disagreements often involve whether a code item was required, whether it relates to covered damage, or whether the policy includes enough ordinance or law coverage. Helpful steps include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Get written confirmation of the requirement from the City or a licensed professional.</li>
<li>Ask your contractor to itemize code-required work.</li>
<li>Compare the insurer's estimate line by line with the permitted scope.</li>
<li>Submit a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> with the documentation, or request an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">independent estimate review</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the dispute is over the amount of loss, your policy's <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal clause</a> may apply. For coverage disputes or legal deadlines, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Confirm requirements with the City before work</h2>
<p>This page reflects our review of City sources as of October 3, 2026. Before starting any repair, contact the City of McAllen Building Permits &amp; Inspections department at (956) 681-1300 or visit the <a href="https://www.mcallen.net/departments/permits/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">department's website</a> to confirm permit, code, and inspection requirements for your project. If you have questions about how those requirements affect your claim, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
